# Source 38: the generic unary map, split nine ways

`Definability/UnaryGen.lean` reduces `Equation38` to a bare set with a unary map, `x ◇ y = s x` for
`s v := v ◇ v` arbitrary.  The `∅`-definable binary operations are the twelve-pattern decision trees
over `x, s x, y, s y`, so `QFOp.definable_graph` hands over the forward half of `StructuralOnMagma`,
and since every magma carries every one of these trees the reverse half is a matter of choosing a
formula.

What is scarce here is the *other* half.  Over the whole model class only the two projections
satisfy any of the fifty targets left open below 38, and a projection names nothing back.  But
`StructuralFrom` is proved model by model, so a `by_cases` on any property of `M` is legitimate --
the operation, the defining formula and the satisfaction proof may all differ per branch -- and on a
branch the class of models is smaller, so satisfaction is easier.  They *have* to differ: the
branches below partition every model, so a tree satisfying the target on all nine satisfies it
outright, and only a projection does that.

The nine leaves are the cells of six predicates on `s`, each of them `∅`-definable in the source
because `s v` is `v ◇ v`:

* **`s = id`.** Then `x ◇ y = x` and `M.Graph` is the pure-equality set `z = x`, definable in any
